er is exposing or ridiculing the situation, implying the person is not as honest as they claim, thus mocking them. The act of telling the story to the reverend serves to bring attention to the situation, which aligns with "let the police notice him" (or at least let the authority figure notice the questionable transaction). Given the theatrical and slightly accusatory delivery common in musical storytelling of this nature, mockery/exposure is the most fitting intention.\n\n**Step 4: Conclusion.**\nThe speaker is recounting a suspicious transaction (receiving silver after claiming hardship) to a figure of authority (the reverend) in a way that sounds sarcastic or disbelieving. This points towards mocking the subject of the story and drawing attention to their actions.\n\nAnswer: $\boxed{D}$
