2025-12-24 15:39:22 | INFO | Q264 id=fBMli2YAR8k_00-11-40_00-12-10 | baseline_pred=C | gold=C | prompt_variant= | followup_pred=C | followup_correct=True | raw=The user is asking me to identify the psychoacoustic effect illustrated by the audio, based on the provided text.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n1.  **Analyze the text:** The speaker explicitly compares the phenomenon in question to the "blind spot of the human eye."\n2.  **Describe the eye's blind spot:** The text states that at the blind spot, "We can't see anything there, but our brain fills in the details for us. It invents an image where there is nothing."\n3.  **Relate to hearing:** The speaker then says, "Our ear do the same thing."\n4.  **Describe the experiment:** The speaker asks the listener to listen to the sentences again, but this time the background sounds (which were mixed in the first time) will be removed. This implies that when the background sounds were present, the listener perceived the full sentence, even where the background sounds covered parts of the speech.\n5.  **Identify the corresponding auditory effect:** The phenomenon where the brain perceives a complete phoneme or word even when part of the acoustic signal is masked or missing is known as the **phoneme restoration effect**. This effect is analogous to the visual filling-in that occurs at the blind spot.\n\n**Conclusion:** The description directly matches the definition of the phoneme restoration effect, where the brain "invents" missing auditory information based on context, similar to how it fills in the visual blind spot.\n\nAnswer: $\boxed{C}$
